  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 300 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nSunday through Friday:\n\nClearing skies from west to east into Sunday as remnants of \nIsaias move eastward into the Northeast U.S., with highs in the\nupper 70s to low 80s expected (highest temperatures in the west\nwhere skies clear earliest). Southerly winds continue, but\nlighter as the low to the west weakens and lifts northward.\nOvernight, expect lows in the upper 50s to low 60s.\n\nPattern begins to break down Monday through the week, with broad\nhigh pressure over the Southeast U.S. and multiple shortwaves\npropagating over the northern Plains into the northern Great\nLakes. Isolated to scattered showers and storms are possible\nMonday afternoon and night (20-40%) as a stronger shortwave\ninteracts with a broad warm frontogenesis region. Highs in the \nupper 70s to low 80s and lows in the 50s. A brief period of dry \nconditions expected Tuesday into Tuesday night as high pressure \nbuilds in behind the passing shortwave/cold front combo, with \nnortherly winds. Expect highs in the 60s, lows in the 40s. \n\nBroad low pressure persists over the central Plains Wednesday\nthrough Friday, with its warm front expanding eastward across\nthe southern Great Lakes region into Wednesday afternoon,\nscattered showers and storms and increasing southeasterly winds\nare expected. Low pressure ejects into the Upper Great Lakes\nThursday into Thursday night, bringing widespread rain/storm\npotential. Low pressure exits to the northeast into Friday, with\na cold front sweeping through the region and bringing gusty\nnorthwesterly winds Friday into Friday night. Lows in the 30s\nare possible Friday night, and isolated pockets of frost may \ndevelop.\n\nMH",
